<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paris Commune</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Commune de Paris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Commune was a French revolutionary government that seized power in Paris on 18 March 1871 and controlled parts of the city until 28 May 1871. After the French Army's defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>, soldiers of the National Guard took the city and refused the authority of the Third Republic under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolphe-thiers">Adolphe Thiers</a>. The Commune separated church and state, remitted rents and abolished child labor. The army suppressed it in the semaine sanglante, the bloody week beginning on 21 May, killing in battle or executing an estimated 10,000 to 15,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communards">Communards</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> saw it as the first example of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris-commune.jpg" alt="Paris Commune" width="330" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barricade18March1871_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 2 September 1870 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was defeated at the Battle of Sedan and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> was captured. Republican deputies proclaimed a Republic and formed a Government of National Defence, and the German army had surrounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> by 20 September. Paris workers had long demanded that the city have its own elected council, and during the siege demonstrations demanded a Commune.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> faced famine and the armies were defeated, national elections on 8 February 1871 returned about 400 deputies favouring a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>. The National Guard in Paris had not been disarmed and had on paper 260 battalions. On 18 March two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> climbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montmartre">Montmartre</a> to seize its 170 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, which had been paid for by public subscription, but National Guard members gathered there, and the Guard seized control of the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Commune held its first meeting on 28 March and voted among other things to abolish the death penalty and military conscription. On 2 April it declared the separation of church and state, seized the property of religious congregations and ordered Catholic schools to become secular. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vendome-column">Vendôme Column</a> honouring Napoleon I was pulled down on 16 May.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolphe-thiers">Adolphe Thiers</a> assembled a new army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-de-macmahon">Patrice de MacMahon</a>, and fighting began on the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in late March. Both sides shot prisoners, and on 5 April the Commune passed a Decree on Hostages. On 21 May fifty thousand soldiers entered the city through an unguarded section of the wall. Fighting went on street by street through the semaine sanglante, and the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communards">Communards</a> were shot at Père Lachaise cemetery on 27 May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The army officially recorded 43,522 prisoners, and the Commune had executed the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, Georges Darboy, and about one hundred hostages in its final days. The Third Republic tried 15,895 prisoners and found 13,500 guilty. Ninety-five were sentenced to death, 251 to forced labour and 1,169 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a>, usually to New Caledonia. All surviving prisoners and exiles were pardoned in 1880. One unconfirmed estimate from 1876 put the toll of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communards">Communards</a> at 20,000.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> drew lessons from the Commune, and Engels called it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a>. Anarchists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-bakunin">Mikhail Bakunin</a> supported it as a rebellion against the state. The wall at Père Lachaise where prisoners were shot is called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communards">Communards</a>' Wall and is the site of annual commemorations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paris Commune” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Paris, workers and National Guards rose and took power as the <b>Paris Commune</b>, which the army under Patrice de MacMahon crushed in the Bloody Week of May 1871.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Paris Commune</b> of 1871 was crushed with thousands of deaths.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Peter Kropotkin’s influence anarcho-communism, inspired by the <b>Paris Commune</b> of 1871, became the dominant view by the late 19th century, and in 1889 Fernando Tarrida del Mármol called for an “anarchism without adjectives” to unite the schools.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Between capitalism and communism stands the dictatorship of the proletariat, based on the model of the <b>Paris Commune</b> and meant to be the most democratic of states, with all public authority elected and recallable.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>Napoleon III's government brought it back to rally the country in the Franco-Prussian War, and the <b>Paris Commune</b> of 1871 sang it with new words.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a></li><li><blockquote>The song came to be closely linked with the <b>Paris Commune</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/le-temps-des-cerises">Le Temps des cerises</a></li></ul></section>
